;; bedit — a super-minimal GUI text editor, built on the rope-backed buffer
;; framework (editor/buffer) + the ui-run loop (editor/ui). The M2 editor app:
;; a standalone `nest` project that `(:use editor/buffer)`s the bundled editor
;; framework. See src/main.blsp.
;;
;; Run it (needs a GUI-enabled `nest` — `./configure --with-gui && make install`
;; in the brood repo):
;;     nest run                 ; a scratch buffer
;;     nest run -- notes.txt    ; open (Ctrl-S saves) that file
;;
;; The editor depends only on bundled std modules — `net/*` (web mirror) and
;; `supervisor` (the logger supervisor) ship in the default `nest` install
;; (ADR-097, batteries-included), so there are no external source-paths.
(project
  :name "bedit"
  :version "0.4.2"
  ;; The floor is what bedit actually needs, not the version it was first written on:
  ;; `os/spawn-pty` (the project REPL under a pty, src/bshell.blsp) and `sort`'s
  ;; comparator-last order both arrived in 0.26.0, and 0.27.1 is the first release whose
  ;; checker narrows `filter` on a type predicate — which is what makes `nest check` (the
  ;; CI gate) clean on the failure-value idiom this code is written in.
  ;;
  ;; Ahead of the floor: the byte-size columns call `humanize/*`, a std module that landed
  ;; on brood main after 0.27.2 and so has no released version to name yet. Raise this to
  ;; `>= 0.28.0` when brood cuts that release — until then a `nest` built from main is what
  ;; runs bedit, and an older one fails on `humanize/byte-size` rather than on this line.
  :brood ">= 0.27.1"
  :kind :app ; an application you run, not a library you depend on (hive listing)
  :description
    "An Emacs-style editor written in Brood: rope-backed buffers, rebindable keymaps, modes, LSP, git, and an eval sandbox — pure Brood glue over the std/editor toolkit and the ui-run loop."
  :repository "https://github.com/broodlang/bedit"
  ;; No build dependencies: bedit is pure glue over bundled std. Theme (and other) plugins
  ;; are installed at RUNTIME into ~/.config/bedit/packages via M-x package-list — not here.
  :source-paths ["src"])
